Using only the configured wiki at WIKI_PATH=/root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki.

Trace: completed task → reusable knowledge → later-session reuse

1. Hermes completes a task.
   This is the first stage of the “learning loop”: “task execution.” The wiki describes Hermes as a self-improving agent whose core differentiator is turning experience into reusable skills and a cross-session user model.
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/learning-loop.md
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/entities/hermes-agent.md

2. Hermes evaluates the outcome.
   The next learning-loop stage is “outcome evaluation.” The wiki does not give implementation details for exactly how success/failure is scored, but it explicitly names outcome evaluation as part of the five-stage loop.
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/learning-loop.md

3. Hermes extracts procedural knowledge.
   The third learning-loop stage is “skill extraction.” The extracted knowledge becomes procedural memory in the skills system: named reusable reasoning patterns stored as markdown SKILL.md files.
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/learning-loop.md
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/skills-system.md

4. Hermes stores that knowledge as a skill.
   The concrete storage mechanism is the skills system:
   - skill = folder with SKILL.md
   - location = ~/.hermes/skills/
   - format = YAML frontmatter plus sections such as description, When to Use, Procedure, Pitfalls, Verification
   - compatible with the agentskills.io open standard
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/skills-system.md

5. The skill can be created through several mechanisms.
   The wiki names four creation paths:
   - hand-written skill
   - /learn
   - agent auto-save after solving
   - install from the Skills Hub

   For the “Hermes completes a task” path specifically, the relevant mechanism is “agent auto-save after solving.” The /learn mechanism can also capture source material such as a directory, docs URL, past conversation, or pasted notes into a skill.
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/skills-system.md

6. Hermes refines the skill.
   The fourth learning-loop stage is “skill refinement.” The Hermes page also says Hermes “improves [skills] with use,” and the learning-loop page says skills are improved during use.
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/learning-loop.md
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/entities/hermes-agent.md

7. Hermes keeps cross-session context separately as memory / user model.
   In parallel with skills, the learning loop builds a “compounding user model across sessions.” The wiki names:
   - agent-curated memory
   - periodic nudges
   - user modeling
   - preserved preferences
   - preserved decision history
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/learning-loop.md
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/entities/hermes-agent.md

8. In a later session, Hermes retrieves relevant knowledge.
   The fifth learning-loop stage is “skill retrieval.” The skills system uses progressive disclosure: a small skill index loads first, and full skill content loads only when needed. This lets Hermes identify and reuse the relevant SKILL.md without loading everything.
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/learning-loop.md
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/skills-system.md

9. Hermes can also recall prior-session information.
   The wiki names “FTS5 cross-session recall with LLM summarization” as a mechanism in the learning loop. That is distinct from skill retrieval: skills are procedural memory, while FTS5 recall + summarization supports cross-session recall of past context.
   Cite: /root/hermes-brain/deploy/wiki/concepts/learning-loop.md

Mechanisms involved, as named in the wiki:

- learning loop
- task execution
- outcome evaluation
- skill extraction
